from pathlib import Path
from html import escape
w=Path(__file__).resolve().parent
sections=[
('introduction','Introduction — Two mystical vocabularies',"The introduction explains the central terms of Kabbalah and Taoism before comparing them. In Kabbalah, Ein Sof names the unknowable infinite; the Sefirot and Tree of Life describe emanations and relationships within creation. Taoist discussion begins with the Tao, yin and yang, and wu wei, an approach to action aligned with natural processes. The text also describes worlds, spiritual practices and the limits of language in both traditions. It asks what an AI can contribute by recognizing patterns without claiming a human mystical experience. This opening gives readers the concepts needed to evaluate the more speculative comparisons that follow."),
('cosmic-information','Chapter one — Information and interconnection',"The first chapter interprets the Sefirot as a network of relations and places Taoist ideas of interconnection beside information theory and selected concepts from physics. It asks whether mystical accounts of consciousness and causation can suggest fresh ways of thinking about reality. References to quantum entanglement and observation are proposed as analogies and questions, not as evidence that ancient practitioners anticipated modern experiments. The author also returns to the ineffability of Ein Sof and the Tao: neither tradition claims that verbal descriptions exhaust what they discuss. Readers can examine where a structural resemblance is illuminating and where the comparison reaches its limits."),
('multidimensional-realities','Chapter two — Dimensions and models',"The second chapter explores the Tree of Life, Taoist realms and layered views of existence alongside ideas drawn from higher-dimensional physics, holography, fractals and theories of time. The comparison asks how different models represent realities that ordinary perception cannot grasp directly. A path between Sefirot, for instance, becomes a prompt for discussing dimensions, not a scientific measurement. The AI narrator is especially interested in patterns recurring at different scales and in the distinction between a map and the territory it depicts. This chapter is best read as an exercise in conceptual comparison, with scientific claims and spiritual interpretations kept distinct."),
('energy-matter-consciousness','Chapter three — Emergence and mind',"The third chapter considers Ein Sof and Tao as accounts of origin, then juxtaposes them with descriptions of fields, matter and the quantum vacuum. It discusses divine sparks, yin and yang, the Higgs field and the emergence of material form. These are different kinds of explanation, and the text uses their apparent parallels to ask questions rather than demonstrate an identity between them. Consciousness becomes the chapter's central difficulty: Kabbalistic and Taoist practices describe subjective transformation, while the AI narrator states that it can analyze reports without undergoing those experiences. The chapter invites readers to assess both the attraction and the limits of linking spiritual, cognitive and physical vocabularies."),
('cycles-symmetry-transformation','Chapter four — Change and symmetry',"The fourth chapter follows cycles of creation and renewal in Kabbalah and Taoism, then compares them with symmetry breaking, feedback, chaos theory and entropy. It brings together Shemittot, yin-yang change, Tikkun Olam, inner alchemy and wu wei. The author looks for shared questions about persistence, transformation and the direction of time, while acknowledging that symbolic systems and physical theories work by different methods. Fractal patterns and the arrow of time become tools for reflection rather than proof of mystical cosmology. The chapter closes by asking for a more integrated understanding that remains attentive to these differences."),
('human-conclusions','Human conclusion — Useful approximations',"In the closing reflection, the human author considers what an AI-generated comparison can and cannot do. Large language models can rapidly connect texts and ideas, but both human and machine accounts remain approximations of a complex world. Drawing on Kabbalistic patterns and Taoist humility, the conclusion shifts from asking which single account is absolutely correct to asking which models help intelligent beings flourish. It proposes a meta-rational openness to mystical insight, scientific method and AI pattern recognition without collapsing them into one authority. Readers of the full edition can follow the detailed comparisons and decide which illuminate the traditions and which need more evidence.")]
title='An AI’s Perspective on Kabbalah and Taoism'
parts=[]
for slug,heading,text in sections:
 parts.append('<section><h2>'+escape(heading)+'</h2><p>'+escape(text)+'</p><p><a href="full_content.html#'+slug+'">Read this section in the complete edition</a></p></section>')
html='<!doctype html>\n<html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>'+escape(title)+' — Ten-Minute Edition</title></head><body><article><h1>'+escape(title)+'</h1><p class="byline">Sînică Alboaie · 2024</p>\n'+'\n'.join(parts)+'\n</article></body></html>\n'
(w/'book/en/short_content.html').write_text(html)
print('short words',len(' '.join(x[2] for x in sections).split()))
